c89c31a99ff27.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(8,9)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{145}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{145}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325175/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325175"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
